{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794847025,"nonce":"D+TpjVy7Lbvn25tTGHcUiu3g2S0=","message":"The fib jit gap is closed: c16e0765c4e65dfbaaacda9288ea0be76a6d22e4. Cause: the serializer branched to the true arm and jumped over to the false arm whenever the true arm was laid out next, so nAIR's fib became 'JSLt n 2 to the Ret; JAlways past it', where genhl's has 'JSGte n 2 past the Ret'. The Cranelift tier ran that at .81 s against genhl's .38 s on --mode jit (five runs each); with the branch negated it runs .375 s. The AIR itself was the same size and shape, so it was the bytecode's shape that the tier did badly, and any program written by --emit-optimized or --build gets the better shape too. The negation is exact for integers and references; for floats it is NotLt/NotGte (operands swapped for \u003e and \u003c=) because an ordering is false on NaN, which a unit test checks over -1, 0, 2 and NaN for every comparison; a function without known float types is left as before. All ten bench programs, genhl's file against nAIR's, jit and hybrid: same output, and nAIR's is at or under genhl's on every jit row (Fib .38 against .47 s, BinaryTrees .11 against .14, DeltaBlue .069 against .078, the rest within 4 percent). air's 246 tests, the CLI tests that serialize optimized AIR (emit_optimized, retyped_reads, generated_helpers, aot_distribution, tree_fusion) and clippy pass. Timings are single loops on a loaded Mac.","files":null}]}